WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.835
Validation supports SQL injection
prevention by reducing ambiguity before data

2
00:00:03.915 --> 00:00:05.171
reaches query-building code.

3
00:00:05.251 --> 00:00:07.039
It does not replace parameterized queries,

4
00:00:07.119 --> 00:00:08.907
but it makes the database boundary
clearer.

5
00:00:08.987 --> 00:00:11.017
A request should be decoded into expected
fields,

6
00:00:11.097 --> 00:00:12.402
converted into useful Go types,

7
00:00:12.482 --> 00:00:15.768
and checked against the rules that apply
to the operation before the application

8
00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.201
builds database calls around it.

9
00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:19.601
Good validation checks more than whether a
value exists.

10
00:00:19.681 --> 00:00:22.629
It checks type, length, range, allowed
values, required combinations,

11
00:00:22.709 --> 00:00:23.627
and business meaning.

12
00:00:23.707 --> 00:00:26.896
A date range may parse correctly but still
be too wide for a reporting endpoint.

13
00:00:26.976 --> 00:00:28.813
A sort option may be present but
unsupported.

14
00:00:28.893 --> 00:00:32.034
A status value may be spelled correctly
but invalid for the current workflow.

15
00:00:32.114 --> 00:00:34.820
These checks should happen before query
logic depends on the data.

16
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.495
Normalization is the companion habit.

17
00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:39.475
Applications often trim whitespace,
canonicalize enum-like values,

18
00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:42.889
parse numeric fields, and convert external
request shapes into internal models.

19
00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:46.207
After normalization, downstream code
should receive narrow values with clear

20
00:00:46.287 --> 00:00:46.674
meaning.

21
00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:49.991
That makes it harder for a later function
to misunderstand whether a field is raw

22
00:00:50.071 --> 00:00:52.584
input, a validated choice, or a trusted
internal identifier.

23
00:00:52.664 --> 00:00:55.419
Allowlist validation is especially useful
for dynamic structure.

24
00:00:55.499 --> 00:00:57.190
Sort options, categories, report types,

25
00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.735
and selected views should map to known
application choices.

26
00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:02.908
Unsupported choices should be rejected
with a safe, predictable response.

27
00:01:02.988 --> 00:01:05.743
Silent fallback can hide mistakes and
create surprising behavior,

28
00:01:05.823 --> 00:01:07.852
so it should be used carefully and
intentionally.

29
00:01:07.932 --> 00:01:11.122
Even after strong validation, runtime
values should still be parameterized.

30
00:01:11.202 --> 00:01:14.150
Validation and parameterization solve
different parts of the problem.

31
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:17.081
Validation decides whether the request is
meaningful and supported.

32
00:01:17.161 --> 00:01:20.302
Parameterization preserves the boundary
between SQL structure and values.

33
00:01:20.382 --> 00:01:23.620
Together they create code that is safer,
easier to test, and easier to maintain.
